	// F: j x l  = C (j x m) * D (m x l)
	// G: i x l  = E (i x j) * F (j x l)
	//
	// The code below keeps the mathematical structure:
	//   E[i,j] = sum_k A[i,k] * B[k,j]
	//   F[j,l] = sum_m C[j,m] * D[m,l]
	//   G[i,l] = sum_j E[i,j] * F[j,l]
	//
	// Compared to the original kernel, we:
	//   - Use scalar accumulators in registers for each output element,
	//     so E/F/G are written exactly once per element.
	//   - Choose memory layouts so that in each reduction both operands
	//     are accessed in unit stride along the reduction dimension:
	//       * Stage 1: A[i,k] and B[k,j] contiguous in k
	//       * Stage 2: C[j,m] and D[m,l] contiguous in m
	//       * Stage 3: E[i,j] and F[j,l] contiguous in j
	//   - Keep all loop nests expressed via Noarr traversers.

